WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.899
The write-up is part of the system.

2
00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:06.403
By week 6 you have built something that works, measured how well, and hardened it.

3
00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:10.459
None of that reaches anyone unless the write-up and demo carry it.

4
00:00:10.739 --> 00:00:23.155
That is not a soft skill bolted on at the end: in real teams, the report is how a system gets adopted, funded, or stopped, and a reviewer who can’t follow your evidence will assume it isn’t there.

5
00:00:23.435 --> 00:00:37.749
This lesson covers four things: writing one report that serves two very different readers, presenting eval results without overclaiming, recording a demo that shows evidence rather than polish, and answering the hard questions in a defense.

6
00:00:38.029 --> 00:00:46.179
You already have the raw material: the scope doc from week 1, the experiment log, the eval table, the risk register, and the traces.

7
00:00:46.459 --> 00:00:48.784
The job now is selection and honesty.

8
00:00:49.064 --> 00:00:54.291
Google’s free Technical Writing courses (One and Two) are the main writing reference here.

9
00:00:54.571 --> 00:00:59.840
They are written for engineers, short, and licensed so that this lesson can adapt their advice.

10
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:09.805
If you have an afternoon, take Technical Writing One in full; its units on audience, documents, and paragraphs pay off in every report you write after this one.
